    "type": "question",
    "label": "12",
    "prompt": "Prove that no three consecutive integers are in geometric progression.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "No three consecutive integers are in geometric progression.\n\nFor consecutive integers $n,n+1,n+2$, the necessary condition $(n+1)^2=n(n+2)$ gives $1=0$, a contradiction.",
      "worked": "Let the consecutive integers be $n,n+1,n+2$. If they were in geometric progression, the square of the middle term would equal the product of the outer terms:\n$$\\begin{align*}(n+1)^2&=n(n+2)\\\\n^2+2n+1&=n^2+2n\\\\1&=0.\\end{align*}$$\nThis contradiction proves that no three consecutive integers are in geometric progression.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 42
  },
